(Prolonged Oral Anticoagulation Versus Standard Duration for Low-Risk Thromboembolic Events) Tj
T*
(in Cancer: Subgroup Analysis by Clinical Functional Status) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Extended anticoagulation may reduce thrombotic recurrence in cancer patients, but the) Tj
T*
(optimal strategy across different functional statuses requires evaluation. We analyzed 178) Tj
T*
(cancer patients with low-risk PE stratified by performance status: 79 with preserved) Tj
T*
(functional capacity and 99 with functional limitations. The prolonged-therapy group \(18) Tj
T*
(months\) versus standard-therapy group \(6 months\) showed markedly different recurrent) Tj
T*
(thrombosis rates in the functional-capacity stratum \(3.1% vs. 19.2%; p=0.048\) but a trend) Tj
T*
(without statistical significance in the functionally-limited stratum \(8.0% vs. 18.9%;) Tj
T*
(p=0.11\). Major bleeding was comparable in the functional-capacity group \(2.5% vs. 7.4%;) Tj
T*
(p=0.39\) but appeared numerically elevated with prolonged therapy in the functionally-) Tj
T*
(limited group \(11.8% vs. 4.1%; p=0.22\). Results suggest clinical benefit from extended) Tj
T*
(treatment in patients with better baseline function, while increased bleeding risk) Tj
T*
(warrants caution in those with functional decline.) Tj
